_irNotes: {
	"decision-01": "The benchmark is the oracle, and it is reproduced rather than interpreted. Element ids (run, runlots, add, update, clear, swaprows), class names (test-data, lbl, remove, danger), the four-cell row and the button captions are the published implementation's. A nicer arrangement of them would cost the app the only thing it is for: being recognisable to anyone who has read any other implementation of the same benchmark"
	"decision-02": "Labels are a walk through the lattice, not a sample from it. The reference implementation draws each of the three words with Math.random, which a compartment does not have. Each index here advances by its own stride — one, seven and five, each coprime to the list it walks — so consecutive labels differ in all three words and the walk is 3,575 long before it returns to its start. It yields 3,250 distinct labels rather than 3,575, because the colour list holds \"brown\" twice; that repetition is the benchmark's and is kept. The output has the shape of a sample and the app replays, which is worth more here than sampling was"
	"decision-03": "A click carries ids, so the buttons wear them. A handler event is the row's id and the element's own DOM id, with no payload. Selecting a row needs only the first. The two buttons that also speak to the reduce carry id=\"run\" and id=\"runlots\" and are told apart by the second — which is what those attributes are for in the benchmark anyway"
	"decision-04": "Position is a column, and a swap exchanges it. A region draws its rows in the order a column names, so the table carries ord and Swap Rows gives two rows each other's value. The observable move is the array swap every other implementation performs — the rows at positions 1 and 998 change places and their ids travel with them — and it costs two writes rather than a thousand"
	"decision-05": "Removing is the fold's, not a form's. A reduce states three things about a row — that it is this, that these fields of it become that, or that its key has no row — so every one of this benchmark's nine operations is one fold, and none of them needs a form. Clear states the absence of every row it can see. The row's ✕ states the absence of one, and carries which in its own DOM id, because the label beside it carries the same row and a click says nothing else about which was hit. Create-1,000 and Create-10,000 state both halves at once, the absences and the thousand arrivals in one array, so no counter has to keep a refill clear of its own deletion. The control region is what the benchmark's is, six buttons and no form, and the table's only other element is the label"
	"decision-06": "The selected row's class is a column. A template binds a field and cannot compose one, so cls holds the literal word danger and the row binds class=\"{cls}\". Selecting writes at most two rows — the one that gains the class and the one that loses it — because restating the other nine hundred and ninety-eight would be a thousand mutations saying nothing"
	"decision-07": "A row arrives and leaves at once. The preset gives every list an enter and an exit keyframe, and a row on its way out stays in the list until its exit has played. The benchmark's rows play nothing, and its oracle times a removal to the row being gone, so the screen rebinds both slots to no animation — the binding the preset declares rebindable for exactly this. Removing a row is then the fold, the store and one frame, which is what the reference measures too"
	"decision-08": "Escape hatches: none. No container, no WASM unit, no vendored component, no external endpoint: hatches and vendored are empty and blobs is false, so the program's cluster unification and the emitted bayt.cue redeclaration carry no changes. The one piece of app code is the bench fold, a compartment the terminal already runs for any handler. The point of this app is to measure the terminal's write contract, and a benchmark that reached outside the terminal would be measuring something else"
}

